WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.631
Laurie was in Grandpa’s study, looking for a book she needed for her history report.

2
00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:12.632
"Aha!" she said, grabbing a book called Alexander the Great

3
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:16.831
. Just then a leather-bound journal caught her

4
00:00:16.832 --> 00:00:21.017
eye. When she pulled it from the shelf, a photograph fluttered to the

5
00:00:21.018 --> 00:00:29.471
floor. It was a photo of Grandpa standing among mysterious stone doorways. Grandpa looked very

6
00:00:29.472 --> 00:00:35.027
young. The title page of the journal read: Persepolis,

7
00:00:35.028 --> 00:00:40.849
1979 . It was written in Grandpa’s handwriting. Laurie turned the page and

8
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:44.274
started reading. "'Jim and I just arrived in Iran

9
00:00:44.275 --> 00:00:48.160
this morning. The drive from the airport was uneventful

10
00:00:48.161 --> 00:00:55.168
but hot. I spent the whole bus ride wondering what the scenery might have looked like back in

11
00:00:55.169 --> 00:00:59.701
500 BC—’" "Laurie?" Grandpa suddenly appeared in

12
00:00:59.702 --> 00:01:04.236
the doorway. "You scared me!" Laurie cried, slamming the

13
00:01:04.237 --> 00:01:09.868
journal shut. "What are you reading?" he asked. "Ah, my journal

14
00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:16.764
from Iran! Your great-uncle Jim and I went to Persepolis the year it was declared a UNESCO World

15
00:01:16.765 --> 00:01:20.558
Heritage site. We had a terrific time exploring the

16
00:01:20.559 --> 00:01:24.409
ruins there." "What is Persepolis?"

17
00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:34.171
asked Laurie. "It was the capital of the Persian Empire, which was the largest empire in the world from 559 BC to

18
00:01:34.172 --> 00:01:42.454
330 BC. It covered most of the Middle East, from Egypt to India, and was made up of many

19
00:01:42.455 --> 00:01:49.514
different provinces. Although Persian rulers tried to do something impossible—unite the known world under one

20
00:01:49.515 --> 00:01:55.684
power—they were nearly successful because they allowed the conquered people to keep their

21
00:01:55.685 --> 00:02:06.377
freedoms. In fact, some call the Cyrus Cylinder—an ancient Persian document written on a clay cylinder—the first declaration of human

22
00:02:06.378 --> 00:02:12.952
rights. It describes how Cyrus let the people in his empire have freedom of culture and

23
00:02:12.953 --> 00:02:19.793
religion. "The ruler who founded the empire and conquered much of its territory was Cyrus the

24
00:02:19.794 --> 00:02:27.630
Great. Many say he began the age of world-empire building, which would continue with the Greeks and

25
00:02:27.631 --> 00:02:31.991
Romans. He had some good ideas about how to create and control an

26
00:02:31.992 --> 00:02:40.108
empire. For example, he knew it was important to make sure the people you conquered were happy so they would not rise up against

27
00:02:40.109 --> 00:02:51.425
you. So when Cyrus marched his troops into Babylon in 539 BC, he did not burn buildings, steal from people’s homes, or run around killing

28
00:02:51.426 --> 00:02:55.443
people. He became known as a peaceful and generous

29
00:02:55.444 --> 00:03:02.728
ruler. He also freed the Jews who were held captive in Babylon and allowed them to return to

30
00:03:02.729 --> 00:03:11.767
Jerusalem. "Cyrus declared each conquered area a province and asked that all provinces pay him a yearly sum of gold and

31
00:03:11.768 --> 00:03:16.931
silver. In return he promised to protect them from foreign

32
00:03:16.932 --> 00:03:23.446
invaders. This system worked out nicely for Cyrus, and he managed to build a wealthy

33
00:03:23.447 --> 00:03:28.821
empire." "Did Cyrus build Persepolis?" Laurie asked, looking back at the

34
00:03:28.822 --> 00:03:38.964
photo. "No, Persepolis was built by a king named Darius the Great, who ruled from 522 BC to 486

35
00:03:38.965 --> 00:03:44.753
BC. Darius was called the Great because he continued to expand the empire’s

36
00:03:44.754 --> 00:03:51.603
borders, developed an organized system of rule, and improved the kingdom with major building

37
00:03:51.604 --> 00:03:58.755
projects. In order to stay informed about his empire, Darius needed people to travel and report back to

38
00:03:58.756 --> 00:04:04.902
him. So he built a great road, called the royal road, which connected the

39
00:04:04.903 --> 00:04:09.563
provinces. And this was way before the Romans built their famous roads, mind

40
00:04:09.564 --> 00:04:15.235
you. The royal road was useful for both transportation and

41
00:04:15.236 --> 00:04:21.566
communication. "He also finished organizing the provinces—something that was begun during Cyrus’ time.

42
00:04:21.567 --> 00:04:25.753
And he appointed governors to manage each province.

43
00:04:25.754 --> 00:04:31.553
This way he could make sure each province paid him the right amount of gold and silver.

44
00:04:31.554 --> 00:04:38.300
But most famously Darius built a huge palace complex in Persepolis.

45
00:04:38.301 --> 00:04:43.293
This served as the center of government and a guest reception area.

46
00:04:43.294 --> 00:04:50.214
The plans for Persepolis—a city that aimed to impress all visitors with its riches and splendor—were

47
00:04:50.215 --> 00:04:56.937
so grand that Persepolis couldn’t be completed within Darius’ lifetime. It

48
00:04:56.938 --> 00:05:00.642
was actually finished by his son, the next king. There

49
00:05:00.643 --> 00:05:05.247
were palaces, great halls, a treasury, and more." "But

50
00:05:05.248 --> 00:05:12.824
Grandpa, you wrote: ‘It is a shame that Alexander the Great laid waste to this magnificent city. I

51
00:05:12.825 --> 00:05:20.121
can only imagine what Persepolis might look like today, if only it had been preserved as a historic treasure,’" Laurie

52
00:05:20.122 --> 00:05:23.538
interrupted, reading from the journal. "I’m

53
00:05:23.539 --> 00:05:29.448
writing a report on Alexander the Great for homework. What did he do to Persepolis?"

54
00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:34.424
"Well, the Persians and the Greeks eventually fought against each other.

55
00:05:34.425 --> 00:05:40.383
Clashes began during Darius’ time, continued through his son’s rule,

56
00:05:40.384 --> 00:05:48.997
and then finally, around 330 BC, the warrior Alexander the Great invaded and conquered Persia.

57
00:05:48.998 --> 00:05:57.268
When he reached Persepolis, he robbed the treasury, set fire to the palaces, and destroyed the sculptures—especially

58
00:05:57.269 --> 00:06:01.220
any that showed images of Persian kings. His

59
00:06:01.221 --> 00:06:07.771
conquests marked the end of the Persian Empire, and the dawn of the Greek. "Laurie,

60
00:06:07.772 --> 00:06:13.489
have you heard of the Greek historian Herodotus? We

61
00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:22.083
know all this history because Herodotus traveled the Middle East collecting stories and recording information. He

62
00:06:22.084 --> 00:06:28.432
had a curious mind and a great interest in the actions and politics of humans. He

63
00:06:28.433 --> 00:06:35.492
wrote about the Persian Empire and Persian wars in a big book called The Histories .

64
00:06:35.493 --> 00:06:45.403
A great writer, he wrote as if he were telling someone a story face-to-face and often mixed in entertaining stories from his travels.

65
00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:50.840
I wish I could write such interesting histories," Grandpa said.

66
00:06:50.841 --> 00:06:58.303
"But your history stories are just like that, Grandpa!" Laurie said. "They’re always interesting!"

67
00:06:58.304 --> 00:07:05.617
"Thanks, Laurie." Grandpa smiled. "Maybe someday you can help me organize these journal entries.

68
00:07:05.618 --> 00:07:11.317
There’s a lot of history in here," he said, lightly tapping his journal.

69
00:07:11.318 --> 00:07:18.194
"I’d be honored." Laurie grinned. "But for now I think I’m pretty busy with Alexander."

70
00:07:18.195 --> 00:07:25.204
She picked up the book and walked out of the study.

